WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.218 --> 00:00:01.958
- On this episode, I'm
gonna talk about humor,

00:00:01.958 --> 00:00:05.538
billion dollar ideas, a tough patch,

00:00:05.538 --> 00:00:09.191
and I take the ALS Challenge.

00:00:09.191 --> 00:00:14.191
( upbeat theme music)

00:00:21.249 --> 00:00:24.089
What's up everybody?
This is Gary Vaynerchuk,

00:00:24.089 --> 00:00:29.089
and this is Episode Nine
of the #AskGaryVee Show.

00:00:29.929 --> 00:00:32.706
Fun fact about the number nine.

00:00:32.706 --> 00:00:35.485
The number nine is John Hall's number,

00:00:35.485 --> 00:00:39.225
the pot smoking wild
kicker of the late 90's,

00:00:39.225 --> 00:00:43.230
early 2000 New York Jets.

00:00:43.230 --> 00:00:45.730
- [Voiceover] Kyle asks,
"What's an area of life

00:00:45.730 --> 00:00:49.921
that you haven't given
your fullest efforts?"

00:00:49.921 --> 00:00:52.261
- Kyle, great question, and I probably

00:00:52.261 --> 00:00:55.501
was willing to answer it because of how

00:00:55.501 --> 00:00:57.261
good I feel about this question.

00:00:57.261 --> 00:01:00.141
There were two places, 36 months ago,

00:01:00.141 --> 00:01:01.381
that I was struggling in.

00:01:01.381 --> 00:01:04.521
One was kind of the non-profit NGO.

00:01:04.521 --> 00:01:06.744
I was giving my dollars,
but I wasn't giving my time.

00:01:06.744 --> 00:01:08.704
Now I am a very proud member of the board

00:01:08.704 --> 00:01:10.601
of Pencils of Promise, and I've gotten

00:01:10.601 --> 00:01:13.201
more involved with my time
which is the real asset.

00:01:13.201 --> 00:01:16.201
I feel like I'm checking
that box of giving back

00:01:16.201 --> 00:01:18.841
outside of my own family
and things of that nature,

00:01:18.841 --> 00:01:19.981
and so I feel good about that.

00:01:19.981 --> 00:01:21.301
And the other one is my health.

00:01:21.301 --> 00:01:26.122
I would say health was the
clear answer only 35 days ago.

00:01:26.122 --> 00:01:31.062
I guess now, maybe a
little bit more Gary time?

00:01:32.522 --> 00:01:36.179
Between kids and the
businesses, and now working out.

00:01:36.179 --> 00:01:40.059
AJ's been pushing me, he's
got into golf a little bit,

00:01:40.059 --> 00:01:43.858
I don't feel like I've
been able to figure out,

00:01:43.858 --> 00:01:46.419
or crack, or hack, Gary time.

00:01:46.419 --> 00:01:49.638
I guess the Jets do that, but
then that's really September

00:01:49.638 --> 00:01:53.252
to December I get that
Sunday for five, seven,

00:01:53.252 --> 00:01:56.272
eight, nine hours depending
if it's a home or road game.

00:01:56.272 --> 00:01:59.260
I think January through September,

00:01:59.260 --> 00:02:01.100
finding something that's just for me

00:02:01.100 --> 00:02:04.120
that I really enjoy is something

00:02:04.120 --> 00:02:05.921
I should probably figure out.

00:02:05.921 --> 00:02:07.820
But the truth is, it doesn't
feel right to me right now.

00:02:07.820 --> 00:02:10.900
The kids are two and five, I
wanna kinda allocate to that,

00:02:10.900 --> 00:02:13.401
the health, I'm just
prioritizing other stuff.

00:02:13.401 --> 00:02:15.560
Hopefully, next two or
three years, Gary time.

00:02:15.560 --> 00:02:17.449
- [Voiceover] Shai asks, "How does

00:02:17.449 --> 00:02:19.929
humor play a role in business?"

00:02:19.929 --> 00:02:21.559
- Shai, this is a really great question,

00:02:21.559 --> 00:02:24.539
and you've been known to
ask me great questions.

00:02:24.539 --> 00:02:26.439
Oh, you though I forgot about WeWork?

00:02:26.439 --> 00:02:27.959
I actually think it plays a role,

00:02:27.959 --> 00:02:31.159
but I would say it plays a role in the way

00:02:31.159 --> 00:02:32.619
that it plays a role in all of life.

00:02:32.619 --> 00:02:34.069
Humor happens to be one of

00:02:34.069 --> 00:02:37.739
the most attractive personality traits.

00:02:37.739 --> 00:02:40.979
Look at the tragic events of
this week, of Robin Williams.

00:02:40.979 --> 00:02:45.590
The enormous emotion,
an outcry and sadness

00:02:45.590 --> 00:02:48.159
and feelings that have come
from the American public,

00:02:48.159 --> 00:02:50.779
shows you how much a comedian can get

00:02:50.779 --> 00:02:53.419
very deeply ingrained in the world

00:02:53.419 --> 00:02:55.779
and he was one of those greats.

00:02:55.779 --> 00:02:59.719
I honestly use humor, a lot
of you watch my keynotes.

00:02:59.719 --> 00:03:01.239
I like to fancy and think that a lot of my

00:03:01.239 --> 00:03:03.759
keynotes take on a
stand up comedy routine.

00:03:03.759 --> 00:03:05.979
I find myself having the ability to drive

00:03:05.979 --> 00:03:09.999
difficult HR conversations
and selling things

00:03:09.999 --> 00:03:13.255
through to clients or
to the general public

00:03:13.255 --> 00:03:14.675
by leveraging humor.

00:03:14.675 --> 00:03:17.775
I think humor is an
enormously attractive trait

00:03:17.775 --> 00:03:19.775
that is a very important part of business,

00:03:19.775 --> 00:03:22.175
but no more important
than the other things

00:03:22.175 --> 00:03:25.035
that people are very
attracted to in others,

00:03:25.035 --> 00:03:28.215
which is caring, and
empathy, self awareness,

00:03:28.215 --> 00:03:32.775
gratitude, kindness, beauty, it's just

00:03:32.775 --> 00:03:37.704
another core tenant that
if you've got it, use it.

00:03:37.704 --> 00:03:40.513
- [Voiceover] Mike asks, "Is
Jab, Jab, Jab, Right Hook

00:03:40.513 --> 00:03:42.592
coming out in audio book form?"

00:03:42.592 --> 00:03:44.172
- Mike, I've been dodging this

00:03:44.172 --> 00:03:45.713
question on Twitter for months,

00:03:45.713 --> 00:03:48.634
so congratulations for
penetrating Episode Nine.

00:03:48.634 --> 00:03:52.034
The answer is, I was
supposed to do it in December

00:03:52.034 --> 00:03:55.595
of last year, January,
I kind of lollygagged.

00:03:55.595 --> 00:04:00.234
The two full days in the
booth just became unpractical,

00:04:00.234 --> 00:04:05.234
'cause VaynerMedia is exploding, and so...

00:04:07.463 --> 00:04:08.706
I've been watching this guy.

00:04:08.706 --> 00:04:10.705
I've been watching you, Aton!

00:04:10.705 --> 00:04:11.725
(laughter)

00:04:11.725 --> 00:04:14.946
This time, I've got your number.

00:04:14.946 --> 00:04:18.445
(laughter)

00:04:18.445 --> 00:04:21.644
We got him this time, guys, we got him!

00:04:26.035 --> 00:04:27.153
Where was I?

00:04:27.153 --> 00:04:29.532
Don't edit any of this, where was I?

00:04:29.532 --> 00:04:31.133
- [Voiceover] The recording
booth was unpractical.

00:04:31.133 --> 00:04:33.192
- Yeah, it was unpractical, VaynerMedia

00:04:33.192 --> 00:04:36.207
is exploding with characters, and so

00:04:36.207 --> 00:04:40.608
I just haven't gotten to it.

00:04:40.608 --> 00:04:43.007
The truth is, we're thinking about

00:04:43.007 --> 00:04:45.027
having somebody else read it.

00:04:45.027 --> 00:04:47.488
It's just kinda one of these
things that is dragging now,

00:04:47.488 --> 00:04:50.547
and is lending itself to me not doing it,

00:04:50.547 --> 00:04:51.767
which I know is now going to

00:04:51.767 --> 00:04:54.168
get a ton of boos in the comment section,

00:04:54.168 --> 00:04:56.027
and I apologize.

00:04:56.027 --> 00:04:58.389
- [Voiceover] Jason asks,
"What's the hardest thing

00:04:58.389 --> 00:05:00.609
you've had to deal with
in the past five years,

00:05:00.609 --> 00:05:03.469
and how did you get through it?"

00:05:03.469 --> 00:05:06.489
- Jason, that is an intense question.

00:05:06.489 --> 00:05:09.289
Actually, before I answer that question,

00:05:09.289 --> 00:05:12.269
I did a little something
fun for the audience,

00:05:12.269 --> 00:05:13.989
and something powerful, I'd like to think,

00:05:13.989 --> 00:05:15.849
my little contribution to the cause

00:05:15.849 --> 00:05:19.089
earlier this morning, let's take a look.

00:05:19.089 --> 00:05:23.829
- I'm doing Ice Bucket
Challenge for ALS aware,

00:05:23.829 --> 00:05:28.169
and you Gary Vee, tell me if
you wanna accept my challenge.

00:05:28.169 --> 00:05:31.159
#AskGaryVee.

00:05:34.999 --> 00:05:37.197
- Hey Michael, it's Gary Vee,

00:05:37.197 --> 00:05:40.817
and I'm taking the Ice Bucket Challenge.

00:05:40.817 --> 00:05:45.077
Bring it Nate, I know
you're gonna love this.

00:05:45.077 --> 00:05:48.097
Holy Cow, whoooooo!

00:05:48.097 --> 00:05:52.486
I challenge you, VaynerNation, to step up!

00:05:52.486 --> 00:05:54.406
That was interesting this morning.

00:05:54.406 --> 00:05:56.667
Nate has never been happier.

00:05:56.667 --> 00:05:59.867
Let's make sure we edit
in at Nate's handle

00:05:59.867 --> 00:06:01.246
in there so he get's a little love.

00:06:01.246 --> 00:06:04.387
I love when we give the
Vayner peeps some love.

00:06:04.387 --> 00:06:07.066
Now to a little bit more of
a somber, serious question.

00:06:07.066 --> 00:06:10.306
Jason, over the last five
years, the hardest thing

00:06:10.306 --> 00:06:13.606
that I've had to deal with, hands down,

00:06:13.606 --> 00:06:17.526
it has to be the fact of
siphoning off of Wine Library TV,

00:06:17.526 --> 00:06:20.786
Daily Grape, that routine of six years,

00:06:20.786 --> 00:06:23.647
and really leaving the day in, day out

00:06:23.647 --> 00:06:25.846
operations of the family business,

00:06:25.846 --> 00:06:28.026
that is the most emotional business.

00:06:28.026 --> 00:06:32.146
Working with my dad, kind
of this transition of being

00:06:32.146 --> 00:06:33.566
day in and day out with my dad,

00:06:33.566 --> 00:06:35.746
to being day in, day out with my brother,

00:06:35.746 --> 00:06:38.907
enormous emotion comes with that.

00:06:38.907 --> 00:06:42.606
I probably never even realized
that that would ever happen.

00:06:42.606 --> 00:06:44.743
It was a very big brain twist for me,

00:06:44.743 --> 00:06:47.663
that I would be transitioning
out of the family business

00:06:47.663 --> 00:06:50.284
into another chapter of my life,

00:06:50.284 --> 00:06:51.863
and I struggled with that quite a bit.

00:06:51.863 --> 00:06:55.223
A lot of heavy emotion, the relationships

00:06:55.223 --> 00:06:58.863
with the people at Wine
Library, my dad and I dynamics,

00:06:58.863 --> 00:07:01.243
just all these very interesting things

00:07:01.243 --> 00:07:03.824
that was happening right
when Misha was born.

00:07:03.824 --> 00:07:05.183
It was a very interesting time in my life.

00:07:05.183 --> 00:07:08.983
I was growing up, and
transitioning and changing.

00:07:08.983 --> 00:07:12.603
I dealt with it with what
I deal with everything,

00:07:12.603 --> 00:07:15.283
and really, probably the
cornerstone of my happiness.

00:07:15.283 --> 00:07:18.063
I dealt with it by over-communicating.

00:07:18.063 --> 00:07:20.203
The level of communication I had

00:07:20.203 --> 00:07:23.923
with the people involved was very high

00:07:23.933 --> 00:07:26.325
and allowed for it to happen.

00:07:26.325 --> 00:07:29.425
And so, that's what I did.

00:07:29.425 --> 00:07:32.105
- [Voiceover] Damien asks,
"When you have a billion dollar

00:07:32.105 --> 00:07:33.865
potential business plan without the

00:07:33.865 --> 00:07:36.125
financial resources and the inventory,

00:07:36.125 --> 00:07:37.845
where do you start with funding?"

00:07:37.845 --> 00:07:42.845
- Damien, this is one of those questions

00:07:43.605 --> 00:07:46.372
that I like picking for the show,

00:07:46.372 --> 00:07:47.532
because I'm always scared to be

00:07:47.532 --> 00:07:50.532
too rude to the audience.

00:07:50.532 --> 00:07:52.532
You guys are smiling.

00:07:52.532 --> 00:07:56.092
Okay, now I'm freaked out
that Aton's doing stuff.

00:07:56.092 --> 00:07:58.912
Oh, you guys are worried about

00:07:58.912 --> 00:08:00.753
how much I'm about to thrash Damien?

00:08:00.753 --> 00:08:02.752
Got it.
- [DRock] A little bit.

00:08:02.752 --> 00:08:07.752
- Damien, the answer to that
question is very simple.

00:08:08.782 --> 00:08:11.909
We're in a bubble of financing right now.

00:08:11.909 --> 00:08:14.069
There are people that would finance

00:08:14.069 --> 00:08:16.729
this venture just on idea.

00:08:16.729 --> 00:08:17.849
It's happened, people come in

00:08:17.849 --> 00:08:19.089
with a deck, they have nothing.

00:08:19.089 --> 00:08:21.609
People give them money for
a piece of the business,

00:08:21.609 --> 00:08:22.689
and they go on and do it.

00:08:22.689 --> 00:08:25.229
That's your only option,
there's nothing else practical.

00:08:25.229 --> 00:08:27.929
Any time somebody sets up a question...

00:08:29.549 --> 00:08:30.314
Do you know how many people have

00:08:30.314 --> 00:08:31.714
emailed me with the title,

00:08:31.714 --> 00:08:34.464
"About to Help You Buy
the New York Jets," or

00:08:34.464 --> 00:08:35.943
"Gary, Open This Email and You Will

00:08:35.943 --> 00:08:37.126
Buy the New York Jets," or

00:08:37.126 --> 00:08:38.964
"I'm Gonna Help You
Buy the New York Jets,"

00:08:38.964 --> 00:08:42.153
and the next line is, "Hey
Gary, I'm a fan of your work.

00:08:42.153 --> 00:08:44.783
I have a billion dollar idea."

00:08:44.783 --> 00:08:47.823
Ideas are (bleep).
Everybody's got an idea.

00:08:47.823 --> 00:08:48.944
Steve, you have ideas?

00:08:48.944 --> 00:08:50.105
- [Steve] I have all sorts of ideas.

00:08:50.105 --> 00:08:53.466
- I have unlimited ideas.

00:08:53.466 --> 00:08:56.824
If you have the idea that's nice.

00:08:56.824 --> 00:08:58.664
If you don't have the dollars,

00:08:58.664 --> 00:09:00.325
and if you don't have the inventory,

00:09:00.325 --> 00:09:02.584
or even if I have nothing,
and so the only way

00:09:02.584 --> 00:09:05.044
you do something about
that is go and get dollars

00:09:05.044 --> 00:09:07.606
from somebody to, I
assume, get the inventory.

00:09:07.606 --> 00:09:08.804
I don't understand what the inventory

00:09:08.804 --> 00:09:10.344
is in this exact question.

00:09:10.344 --> 00:09:11.625
The reason I'm answering this question

00:09:11.625 --> 00:09:15.025
is for the entire
VaynerNation to understand

00:09:15.025 --> 00:09:17.865
that practicality matters, right?

00:09:17.865 --> 00:09:20.523
Passion, which I'm loaded
with, is great. Right?

00:09:20.523 --> 00:09:24.843
Ideas, transcend the world, great, agreed.

00:09:24.843 --> 00:09:27.422
But, in execution, in practicality,

00:09:27.422 --> 00:09:28.822
is when these things become true.

00:09:28.822 --> 00:09:30.282
That's what matters, that's what

00:09:30.282 --> 00:09:32.402
the people we all look up to have done.

00:09:32.402 --> 00:09:36.222
They've taken from here,
and they've made it happen.

00:09:36.222 --> 00:09:39.642
That is massively important,
and that is something

00:09:39.642 --> 00:09:44.642
I push all of you to start
spending some more time on.

00:09:44.862 --> 00:09:49.663
Question of the day for Episode Nine,

00:09:49.663 --> 00:09:54.663
who in your family inspires you the most?

00:09:56.003 --> 00:09:58.299
Tag them up, give them some love.

00:09:58.299 --> 00:09:59.880
It'a a good thing to do.

00:09:59.880 --> 00:10:04.420
You keep asking questions,
and I'll keep answering them.

00:10:04.420 --> 00:10:09.239
(upbeat theme music)

00:10:09.239 --> 00:10:11.039
On this episode I'm gonna chase that

00:10:11.039 --> 00:10:15.220
employee that's (bleep) with me (laughs).

00:10:15.220 --> 00:10:17.079
- [Steve] Outtake!

00:10:17.079 --> 00:10:22.039
- Psst, secretly I think
the reason I'm even doing

00:10:22.619 --> 00:10:25.436
this show is 'cause I realize
I'm in the midst of this

00:10:25.436 --> 00:10:27.956
health transformation
and I'm kind of excited

00:10:27.956 --> 00:10:30.416
to watch me transform
into the Incredible Hulk

00:10:30.416 --> 00:10:34.207
over the course of the
next couple of years.

00:10:34.207 --> 00:10:36.723
Oh yeah, subscribe!

00:10:36.723 --> 00:10:39.547
(upbeat theme music)

